<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ramon Llull</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1232–<a href="/years/1316">1316</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Raymond Lull</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ramon Llull was a philosopher, mystic and missionary from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a>, born soon after the Aragonese conquest of the island. He wrote over 200 works in Catalan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Arabic, including the novel Blanquerna and the Ars Magna, a method for proving Christian truths to Muslims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. He is considered one of the founders of Catalan literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramon-llull.jpg" alt="Ramon Llull" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francesc Ribalta · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francisco_Ribalta_-_Ramon_Llull_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…literature and learning were rich: James I left the Llibre dels fets, the chronicles of Bernat Desclot, Ramon Muntaner and Peter IV followed, <b>Ramon Llull</b> wrote widely in Catalan, Arabic and Latin, and Valencia produced the poet Ausiàs March and the novel Tirant lo Blanc, printed in 1490.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Catalan culture flourished in Romanesque churches such as Sant Climent de Taüll, the Gothic basilica of Santa Maria del Mar, the works of <b>Ramon Llull</b> and a body of royal chronicles in Catalan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Principality of Catalonia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ramon Llull</b>, philosopher and missionary, was its most famous son.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-majorca">Kingdom of Majorca</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Palma, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.57,2.65&amp;year=1286" aria-label="Palma on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4615 747 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ramon Llull"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5065 1017h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5065 1017h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.57,2.65&amp;year=1286">What was here in 1286? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Long and perilous are the paths by which the Lover seeks his Beloved. They are peopled by cares, sighs and tears. They are lit up by love.&#8221;</p><footer>The Book of the Lover and the Beloved, verse 2 (from Blanquerna, about 1283) &#183; tr. E. Allison Peers, 1923 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/60704/pg60704-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;If we understand not one another’s speech, we may make ourselves understood by love; for in thy song I see my Beloved before mine eyes.&#8221;</p><footer>The Book of the Lover and the Beloved, verse 26 (from Blanquerna, about 1283) &#183; tr. E. Allison Peers, 1923 &#183; spoken by the Lover to a bird singing in the garden &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/60704/pg60704-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I am alone, now that I have seen you and heard you; until now, I was in the company of my Beloved.&#8221;</p><footer>The Book of the Lover and the Beloved, verse 46 (from Blanquerna, about 1283) &#183; tr. E. Allison Peers, 1923 &#183; the Lover's answer to passers-by who asked why he sat alone &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/60704/pg60704-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Whether Lover and Beloved are near or far is all one; for their love mingles as water mingles with wine. They are linked as heat with light; they approach and are united as Essence and Being.&#8221;</p><footer>The Book of the Lover and the Beloved, verse 49 (from Blanquerna, about 1283) &#183; tr. E. Allison Peers, 1923 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/60704/pg60704-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Between Hope and Fear, Love made her home. She lives on thought, and, when she is forgotten, dies. So unlike the pleasures of this world are her foundations.&#8221;</p><footer>The Book of the Lover and the Beloved, verse 16 (from Blanquerna, about 1283) &#183; tr. E. Allison Peers, 1923 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/60704/pg60704-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Swifter is love in the lover’s heart than is the brilliance of the lightning to the eye, or the thunder to the ear.&#8221;</p><footer>The Book of the Lover and the Beloved, verse 37 (from Blanquerna, about 1283) &#183; tr. E. Allison Peers, 1923 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/60704/pg60704-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
